```
and, further down (the other half of the fix):
```ts
    // Navigation — navmesh from the terrain mesh; bots are NavCrowd agents driving controllers.
    const nav = await createNavigationPluginAsync({ locateFile: () => "/recast-navigation.wasm" });
    mark("nav-plugin");
```

These two `await`s are the target of my planned change: I intend to load Havok and the nav plugin concurrently (`Promise.all`) so the recast wasm — the second module, which is where the browser hangs — doesn't serialize behind the ~2MB Havok compile. The `world`/`terrain`/`player` lines in between depend on `hknp`, so the restructure has to keep those after the Havok leg resolves.

POINTER: `scene.fixedDeltaMs = 1000 / PHYSICS_FPS` — the fixed physics timestep; if player/bot movement ever stops stepping, this is the first thing to check (scene252.ts, `main()`).
POINTER: the `mark()` helper + `__initStart` + every `console.warn` stage marker are temporary instrumentation I added to diagnose this hang — they must be deleted before commit per GUIDANCE §0b (scene252.ts, `main()`).
POINTER: `mark("terrain")` printing at +90ms confirms the 1024-body `createFpsTerrain` is fast and synchronous — rules out init cost as the hang, keeping the diagnosis on the recast wasm load (scene252.ts, `main()`).
POINTER: the `cap()` end-cap helper + `tb`/`tt` block is the rewritten `rayVsCapsule` (cylindrical band + two end-cap spheres) — unrelated to the hang, but the place to look if bot hit-detection misbehaves later (scene252.ts, `rayVsCapsule`).
POINTER: `player.staticFriction = 0; player.dynamicFriction = 0.1;` — Quake slide tuned via the controller's public friction fields, not shape masks (scene252.ts, `main()`).

